From c0d063e622ca7afab24771289f2319148d1690fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Day <me@samcday.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:28:35 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] window, plan: add the planner and critical window for
 adopted gadgets

A gadget built by an initramfs to serve the root filesystem over
FunctionFS can not be replaced: FunctionFS instance names are global and
the kernel only links a function into a configuration of its own gadget,
and never while the gadget has a UDC set. Switching modes on such a gadget
means changing its links in place, between an unbind and a rebind, while
the root filesystem may be unreachable.

plan.rs decides such a switch from a snapshot of the gadget, without
touching it: which of usb-signaller's own functions to create, link,
unlink and remove, next to the pinned functions that must never leave.
It refuses modes an adopted gadget does not support yet (anything but
charging_only, developer_mode and tethering_mode), modes outside
allowed_modes, missing or non-ready pins, and linked functions nobody
owns. Developer and tethering share a composition, so switching between
them is a service swap. An exhaustive test runs every combination of
pins, linked functions, existing function directories and target mode
against the invariants: pins are never unlinked or removed, only
usb-signaller's own functions are removed, a leaving mass storage LUN is
force-ejected first, and an unchanged composition needs no rebind.

window.rs runs the unbind, relink and rebind on a dedicated thread,
spawned before memory is locked, using only raw syscalls on file
descriptors and buffers prepared beforehand: no logging, allocation,
exec or await while the gadget is unbound. Every attribute read opens a
new file, since configfs caches values per open file. The ladder never
returns while the gadget is unbound: the target composition, else the
previous one, else the pins alone, retried until it binds, unless
someone else bound it meanwhile.

libc becomes a regular dependency (Fedora: rust-libc-devel).

Nothing uses this yet; the next change adopts gadgets with it.

A panic inside a window aborts the process instead of returning with
the gadget possibly unbound and the transaction lock released.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
